\par\par \b \uc2  Canada Afternoon: Cdn Dlr Pushed Weaker In Volatile Market \b0\par\par\uc2 325 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 1997\par 23:00\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-Dow Jones)--The Canadian dollar was weaker against the U.S. dollar late Thursday in a volatile session that saw the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Canadian dollar a couple of times. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3925 at 3:59 p.m. EST (2059 GMT), from C$1.3851 at 8:00 a.m. EST (1300 GMT), and from C$1.3850 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  One trader in Toronto said when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  around C$1.3910, it seemed to buy just enough to calm the dollar down. The U.S. dollar took off again though, about an hour later, and the bank reentered with purchases of Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reportedly near the C$1.3920-25 area and again in that area late in the day. Analysts said the Bank of Canada's total purchase was likely between $200-$250 million (U.S.). \par \par \uc2  'There was nothing in particular sparking it off,' said Bill Mooney, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Societe Generale in Montreal. Mooney said there was some selling of the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by a couple of Canadian banks early in the session and it just seemed to pick up momentum. \par \par \uc2  But the underlying sentiment, according to Mooney, continues to be uncertainty about Friday's employment report. Investors are concerned a strong employment report in the U.S. could trigger another rise in interest rates and hurt the potential for Canadian investments, which would then offer a relatively lower interest rate. \par \par \uc2  As many analysts have noted, the Bank of Canada still doesn't seem to be in any hurry to raise interest rates north of the border and ,'ultimately the pressure is coming to bear on the Canadian dollar for the negative (interest rate) spreads that we've had for so long,' said Mooney. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 03-04-97 \par \par \uc2  2100GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dt4302f9q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
