\par\par \b \uc2  World  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  -2: Canadian Dlr Falls On Weak Jobs Data \b0\par\par\uc2 362 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Juli 1996\par 21:04\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1996, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Lately, the dollar has tended to move in the opposite direction of bond prices, as traders bet that the lure of resulting higher yields on U.S. assets will make investors buy dollars. Bond-holders sell when they see higher rates on the horizon because existing bonds will be less attractive than those to be issued at higher rates. \par \par \uc2  U.S. key interest rates - the discount and federal funds target rates - are at 5% and 5.25% respectively. The Fed kept them there after a policy-meeting this week. \par \par \uc2  The discount rate is the lending rate of last resort for banks, while the fed funds rate is the rate at which banks lend excess reserves to each other overnight. \par \par \uc2  But with Friday's strong jobs numbers combined with a rise in the National Association of Purchasing Management index and expanding consumer spending, many market-watchers think the Fed will have to raise rates at or before its next monetary policy meeting Aug. 20. \par \par \uc2  Some economists say Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan's semi-annual testimony to the Senate July 18 will give the market a clue to his intentions. \par \par \uc2  'The evidence is there (to raise rates), but I think Greenspan is reluctant to hike rates at this point,' said Bank of Montreal's Benfer. 'I think he has to move though before the next meeting. You can wait for more data, but how much more do you need?' \par \par \uc2  News that the Canadian economy lost 56,000 jobs in June, the largest drop since November 1991, sent the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reeling in expectation the Bank of Canada will have to cut interest rates again. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered later in the day, bolstered by by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and renewed buying from investors. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began to sell U.S. dollars at $1.3665 (Canadian), but U.S. dollar didn't turn around until it hit an intraday high of C$1.3700 around 1345 GMT. It ended global trading at C$1.3677, compared to C$1.3593 late Thursday in London. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 05-07-96 \par \par \uc2  1904GMT \par \par \uc2  1033 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011013ds7505jmp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
