\par\par \b \uc2  Traders/Bank of  \highlight22\uc2 Canada \highlight\uc2  -2: Bank  \highlight22\uc2 Intervening \highlight\uc2  More Heavily Now \b0\par\par\uc2 247 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 Oktober 1995\par 15:32\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-DJ)- The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold U.S. dollars at levels around C$1.3640/50 level to try and stem the slide in the Canadian dollar as \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets worry about the threat of Quebec separation. Some traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  starting around the C$1.3610 area. But one trader said the market was disappointed it wasn't in more forcefully and did little to avoid the triggering of stop/loss orders in the c$1.3615/20 area that pushed the the U.S. dollar up quickly above C$1.3640. One trader said the bank is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  more heavily at these upper levels. "It's going to be a hell of a long week," said one trader at a Canadian bank. The U.S. dollar is trading at C$1.3660 at 1330 GMT, up from C$1.3588 at 1200 GMT and C$1.3530 Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar started to slide by the middle of last week, but came under stiff attack late Thursday after after public opinion polls out of Quebec showed the "Yes" side in the independence campaign slightly ahead of the pro-Canada "No" side. The U.S. dollar was trading at C$1.3365 a week ago. Quebec goes to the polls Oct. 30 to decide on separating from Canada. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 10-23-95 \par \par \uc2  0932EDT \par \par \uc2  4020 4160 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011024dran02ec9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
