\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Canada Intervention \highlight\uc2  -2-: Seen To Establish Orderly Decline \b0\par\par\uc2 205 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 1995\par 18:02\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-DJ)--The Bank of Canada was buying Canadian dollars around C$1.3495, said a trader with a large Canadian bank. The Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under heavy pressure since late Thursday after a poll from the Angus Reid Group showed Quebecers split evenly on the issue of separation from \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and many traders had expected the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  "to establish an orderly decline in the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," as one trader put it. Quebecers will vote on the issue in a referendum Oct. 30, and trade is likely to remain volatile through the early part of next week, depending on the trend in the next opinion polls. Groupe Leger & Leger will release a poll Saturday in the Globe & Mail Prior to the renewed volatility, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had stuck to a tight trading range between C$1.3350 and C$1.3400. \par \par \uc2  Another trader confirmed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was buying Canadian dollars around the C$1.3495 level, and sources have said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is fragile at the moment and very likely to break through the C$1.3500 level. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 10-20-95 \par \par \uc2  1202EDT \par \par \uc2  4020 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011024drak02db4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
