\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian dollar rallies after Quebec poll results. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Malina Delikostadinova \par \uc2 655 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Oktober 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  (All figures U.S. dollars unless specified) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Reuter) - The Canadian dollar soared past 75 cents Tuesday for the first time since February 1994 amid mounting confidence that Quebec voters will reject sovereignty in an Oct. 30 referendum. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the Canadian dollar was propelled by the latest opinion poll, released overnight and conducted by the SOM-Environics agency, which showed that 48 percent of voters in the French-speaking province opposed splitting from Canada, while 39 percent supported it. \par \par \uc2  "The only uncertainty that seems to be left is the margin of victory for the 'No' side," said Andrew Spence, chief economist with Citibank Canada. "More and more people are thinking that the 'No' side is going to win and they don't want to miss out on the value of Canadian dollar assets." \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar surged to 75.32 cents in the morning, prompting direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Traders said the Bank sold Canadian dollars for U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 75.30 cents in an effort to take some of the steam out of the rally. \par \par \uc2  Later profit taking took the Canadian dollar back to 75.13 cents. \par \par \uc2  Canada's Finance Minister Paul Martin also attributed the dollar strength to the poll results, calling them "very conclusive." He told reporters in Ottawa that his views were in line with financial market analysts. \par \par \uc2  Rumours that influential U.S. investor George Soros was in the market buying up to C$300 million, also helped lift the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although a spokesman for Soros declined to comment. \par \par \uc2  "It is the policy of Soros Fund management not to comment on rumours," said Soros spokesman Shawn Pattison. \par \par \uc2  "He (Soros) likes Canada. He's got Canada right. The turmoil in Canada over the last three weeks kept him away but now it looks as though he's back," said one New York-based economist who declined to be named. \par \par \uc2  Economists said the political uncertainty surrounding the Quebec referendum has so far been insufficient to keep investors from buying Canadian dollars. \par \par \uc2  "Politics always creates some volatility, but the lesson from the past is that it is temporary," said Clement Gignac, chief economist of Montreal-based Levesque Beaubien Geoffrion. \par \par \uc2  Gignac noted that while the Canadian dollar may be hit by referendum-related investor nervousness in the near term, the long-term fundamentals for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are very positive. \par \par \uc2  "Fundamentals are showing that this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued. With the fundamentals favourable for commodity prices, favourable for the U.S. dollar and with the (government) downsizing in Canada, we have a recipe for a stronger Canadian dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  Citibank's Spence agreed and predicted that the Canadian dollar is headed toward 76.62 cents after the referendum. \par \par \uc2  Gignac said the Canadian dollar is likely to climb even higher, reaching 77-78 cents over the next nine months to a year, irrespective of the outcome of the Quebec referendum. \par \par \uc2  "I am not a political expert. But based on our experience, the market will return to fundamentals after the referendum and the fundamentals are good," Gignac said, adding that an independent Quebec government would likely focus on fiscal responsibility and debt reduction. \par \par \uc2  Analysts also noted that a strong Canadian dollar will open the door for further interest rate cuts by the Bank of Canada. Such cuts, they said, are unlikely before the referendum is over, but once the Quebec issue has been resolved, interest rates could drop by a full percentage point over the near term. \par \par \uc2  "If that (the Quebec issue) gets resolved positively, I would think that short-term rates could come down probably ... by 100 basis points (1 percentage point)," Bruce Galloway, vice chairman of Royal Bank of Canada, told a treasury management conference in Calgary on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has targeted short-term rates at a range of 6 to 6.5 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dra303ehz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
