\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Canada/Cdn Dlr -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Remains Under Pressure \b0\par\par\uc2 185 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 September 1995\par 19:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-DJ)--The Canadian dollar remains under pressure following two rounds of \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop it up amid selling over concerns about Quebec independence. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last seen selling U.S. dollars at C$1.3555/60 over the lunch hour. While that momentarily helped push the U.S. dollar back down and lift the Canadian dollar, the U.S. dollar reversed course and started to head back up. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar had fallen to around C$1.3544 after the last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , before rising quickly to around C$1.3577 at 1705 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has been under pressure since late last week, when financial markets began realizing the potentially destabilizing effects of a referendum on independence in Quebec Oct. 30. \par \par \uc2  The bank was in the market earlier in the day, selling U.S. dollars between C$1.3530 and C$1.3540, but it provided only temporary respite for the sagging Canadian dollar. -0-. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 13-09-95 \par \par \uc2  1721GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr9d073x9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
