\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian Afternoon: Canada Dlr Recovers But Still Vulnerable \b0\par\par\uc2 282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1995\par 22:29\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-DJ)--The Canadian dollar is slightly stronger late Monday after recovering from heavy early losses, boosted primarily by technical factors, but also helped by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is trading at C$1.3643 at 2021 GMT, down from C$1.3696 at 1200 GMT and C$1.3667 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  After gaining steadily overseas, particularly in Asia, the U.S. dollar hit its intraday high of C$1.3750 early in North America. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar began to show signs of weakness late Friday, which continued during Asian trading on Monday; so by the time the North American markets opened the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reached the 1.3750 area against the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the broker system to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around that level, starting the recovery, but most afternoon gains occured for technical reasons, say analysts. A jump back above the 1.3700 level is only a matter of time for the same technical reasons, according to analysts with MMS International. \par \par \uc2  Some cite growing worries over a possible referendum in Quebec this fall for the Canadian dollar's troubles, but many market participants say losses merely developed momentum in a market left thinly traded with many European players out for the August holidays. \par \par \uc2  Thomas Benfer, director, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the Bank of Montreal in New York, says that selling merely gathered momentum as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed weak on Friday. He says the International Monetary Market (IMM) was selling Monday morning. Later corporate demand helped the Canadian dollar to recover, he says. \par \par \uc2  (MORE) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 31-07-95 \par \par \uc2  2029GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr7v05sbv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
