\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian Bonds -3-: Traders Eye U.S. Economic Reports \b0\par\par\uc2 282 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Juli 1995\par 23:48\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Statistics Canada said the country's economy grew 0.3% in May, on the high side of expectations. Some of the sectors that showed weakness the previous month, such as manufacturing, rebounded in May. While growth may not be strong enough to prevent a negative quarter in the April to June period, growing signs of strength in the economy have damped the expectations of some in the market for more easing by the Bank of Canada. The consensus appears to be that the fate of Canadian interest rates rests with the Canadian dollar. Most economists believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would still like to see lower interest rates, but it is not prepared to let the Canadian dollar crumble. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  showed its resolve by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market Monday morning to help support the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , selling U.S. dollars at C$1.3745. The action helped renew some confidence in the Canadian dollar, as the U.S. dollar fell back to trade late in the day a full cent lower around C$1.3645. \par \par \uc2  The North American bond market is expected to focus on a heavy slate of U.S. economic data later this week, primarily Friday's July employment report, as well as the next week's quarterly Treasury refunding. If Friday's jobs data continue the recent trend of strong economic numbers, the prospects of further easing by the Federal Reserve will be dimmed. That may heighten concerns that Canadian and U.S. interest-rate policies may be diverging, placing more pressure on the Canadian dollar, economists said. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 07-31-95 \par 