\par\par \b \uc2  =Bank Of Canada Rate -2-: Rate Declines May Be On Hold \b0\par\par\uc2 312 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juli 1995\par 18:10\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Another factor behind Tuesday's expected bank rate increase is the severe one-day drop in the Canadian dollar. Last Thursday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lost almost a full cent after the failure of Germany's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut rates drove investors into the Deutsche mark and out of the Canadian and U.S. dollars. The selloff forced the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buy Canadian dollars to try and moderate the decline. That prompted a backup in money market yields that continued after the retail sales data. Three-month treasury bills are now yielding around 6.45%, up six basis points Monday. The three-month bill trading in the when-issued market, the guide for predicting the Bank of Canada's rate, is yielding 6.45%, up seven basis points Monday. Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at Bank of Montreal, believes the Bank of Canada may be approaching a point where it will sit tight on rates and see if the easing in monetary conditions over the past few months is having any impact on the economy. \par \par \uc2  There were signs Monday that the economy may be lifting off the bottom. Manufacturing shipments in May rose 0.7% after three straight months of decline. And although Statistics Canada's leading composite index sliped in June again, some longer-term indicators within the index are "suggesting the worst of the economy's recent lethargy may soon end." "At the moment, we're assuming that we're not likely to see any further marked easing in monetary conditions unless the data starts reversing and points to declines later in the year," said Ferley. But he and other economists believe that short-term Canadian interest rates can still move lower if the Canadian dollar strengthens. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 07-17-95 \par \par \uc2  1210EDT \par \par \uc2  4020 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011024dr7h01pfe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
