\par\par \b \uc2  CDN Bonds -2-: Strong Performance "Does Not A Trend Make" \b0\par\par\uc2 339 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Juni 1995\par 23:10\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  But Rosenberg quickly pointed out that Canadian/U.S. yield spreads are still 50 basis points wider than they were only five months ago, and Thursday's performance "does not a trend make." But expectations are that the Canadian dollar, long the reason that many foreign investors shunned the Canadian bond market, may have stabilized. The Canadian dollar had been under pressure since May, when the Bank of Canada began slowly easing monetary policy in an effort to keep the economy from slipping into recession. Earlier suspicions that the U.S. Fed would keep its interest rates steady fueled fears that short-term rate spreads between Canada and the U.S. would narrow and destabilize the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now with growing signs the Fed may ease, the lowering of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk previously associated with holding Canadian bonds have drawn buyers to the market, said traders. \par \par \uc2  Enthusiasm for lower interest rates prompted the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the markets and sell three-month treasury bills at 6.71% in an effort to slow the rally. The yield on three-month bills fell three basis points to around 7.71%, while three-month bills in the when-issued market were yielding 6.65%, a drop of eight basis points and suggesting a decline in next Tuesday's bank rate of around nine basis points to 6.9%. Many money market traders believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its overnight lending firm at between 7% and 7.5% until the July 5-6 meeting of the FOMC. Friday will see the release of another important piece of U.S. economic data. Durable goods orders for May are expected to rise by about 0.4% after falling 4% in April. Any gains within that magnitude are unlikely to cause the bond markets to cancel its bets on lower rates, said an economist. There are no major releases out of Canada Friday. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 06-22-95 \par \par \uc2  1710EDT \par \par \uc2  4020 4152 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011024dr6m01jbn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
