\par\par \b \uc2  Canadian Bonds Soar On U.S. Jobs Report; T-Bill Sales Seen \b0\par\par\uc2 424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 1995\par 16:43\par \uc2  Capital Markets Report \par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-DJ)--Canadian bond prices are soaring early Friday and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  reportedly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market to slow the rally after a weaker-than-expected U.S. employment report triggered widespread investor bond buying. 7.75s 1996  98.95  Up 0.38  8.42% 8.5s  2000  98.50  Up 0.70  8.85% 9.0s  2004  99.70  Up 0.87  9.03% 9s    2025  98.95  Up 1.30  9.09% CGB March   99.60  Up 1.05 10-Yr Spread To U.S. 10-Yr 1.50 vs 1.53 "We're seeing true buy-side interest in this market, with new money being committed. Flows are really good for the first time in weeks," says a Toronto-based trader. Canadian to U.S. 10-year bond spreads are testing a technical barrier at 150 basis points; spreads were 184 basis points two weeks ago. The cause of all this excitement is Friday's U.S. non-farm payrolls, up by 134,000 in January, well under market expectations. The U.S. unemployment rate rose to 5.7%; it was expected to remain unchanged at 5.4% in January. \par \par \uc2   The employment news is rallying the bond market because it reduced inflationary expectations in the U.S., and calmed recent concerns about another tightening by the Federal Reserve in the near future. Money market traders say the Bank of Canada sold three-month treasury bills early Friday at 7.88% to slow a sharp decline in short-term interest rates. Three-month T-bill yields are now at 7.91% compared with 8.08% late Thursday. The fact that the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is taking steps to hold up short rates is propping up the Canadian dollar. The U.S. dollar is at C$1.4026 early Friday compared with C$1.4057 late Thursday. In Canadian economic news, the Bank of Canada's reserves rose by US$924 million in January to US$13.40 billion. Market expectations were for a drop in reserves to US$11.0 billion. But the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a total of US$2.23 billion in Canada Bills, bringing the level of outstanding bills to US$6.26 billion. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other government \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  reduced the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's reserves by US$1.36 billion in January. The report on reserves had no influence on bond trading. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 02-03-95 \par \par \uc2  1043EST \par \par \uc2  4152 \par \par \uc2 Dokument cm00000020011024dr2300lc7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
