\par\par \b \uc2  Thiessen - Canada -2-: Will Continue  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Support \b0\par\par\uc2 248 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Januar 1995\par 21:05\par \uc2  Dow Jones News Service \par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  MONTREAL -DJ- Bank of Canada Governor Gordon Thiessen said Canada's federal and provincial governments need to produce ''a more sustainable'' fiscal situation in the country. \par \par \uc2  In a news conference, Thiessen said it is important that the ratio of debt to gross domestic product shouldn't be continually rising. Thiessen declined to comment specifically on the upcoming federal budget or on the federal government's goal of lowering the deficit as a percentage of GDP to 3% in two years. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't have a target for the Canadian dollar. But he said the bank will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth fluctuations in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has adequate reserves to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it has recently. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen stressed that he regards \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economic fundamentals as strong. He cited the country's low inflation rate, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's commitment to low inflation, rising productivity, cost control by business and rising primary product prices. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen said concerns over the Quebec independence issue have ''added a degree of uncertainty'' to the situation, making markets more nervous. But he said the level of debt in the country matters more. \par \par \uc2  Thiessen said suggestions that the bank should simply allow the Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drop are misguided, because such an approach could lead to fears of inflation and of depreciation, which would only drive interest rates higher. \par \par \uc2  (END) DOW JONES NEWS 01-19-95 \par \par \uc2  3:05 PM \par \par \uc2 Dokument dj00000020011024dr1j00f1q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
