\par\par \b \uc2  Bank of Canada sets special repurchase at 7.25 pct. \b0\par\par\uc2 208 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO, Jan 17 (Reuter) - The Bank of Canada set special repurchase agreements at 7.25 percent, effectively capping its new target range for Canada's call loan rate at 6.75 percent to 7.25 percent, money market traders said. \par \par \uc2  The move follows an earlier \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  where it set reverse repurchase agreements at 6.75 percent, raising the floor of Canada's call loan rate range by 50 basis points, traders said. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  stabilised the Canadian dollar to around C$1.4205 (US$0.7039) from an earlier nine-year low of C$1.4240 (US$0.7022). \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said the Canadian dollar fell sharply on neagative comments from an influential Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader in the Globe and Mail newspaper. \par \par \uc2  Canadian T-bill yields jumped on the dollar's volatility, with the three-month bill trading when-issued at 7.90 percent from Monday's close of 7.40 percent. \par \par \uc2  This implies a rise of a whopping 96 basis points in the Bank of Canada rate at today's auction, which was set last Tuesday at 7.24 percent. \par \par \uc2  -- Paul Casciato (416) 941-8100 \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr1h06y0x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
