\par\par \b \uc2  Canada dollar tumble forces interest rate rise. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Paul Casciato \par \uc2 417 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 1995\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1995 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  TORONTO (Reuter) - The Canadian dollar plunged to a new nine-year low on growing investor jitters Tuesday, forcing the Bank of Canada to raise interest rates to near two-year highs. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has tumbled three-quarters of a U.S. cent in two days, reaching a low of C$1.4145 (70.69 cents) despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  managed to slow the decline only by raising its target range for a key money market rate, the overnight call loan rate, by 0.5 percentage point. \par \par \uc2  The Canadian dollar has come under pressure due to concern about the country's debt and about the crisis in Mexico. Asian investors were cited as some of the biggest sellers, traders said. \par \par \uc2  "Higher budget deficits and Quebec sovereignty heating up -- there are a combination of things that are not looking favourable for Canada," one Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say the Canadian dollar remains on an overall downward trend, regardless of short-term fluctuations. \par \par \uc2  However, ecconomists disagree whether the move in the call loan rate foreshadowed a long-term trend to higher rates in Canada or was a one-time effort to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "We viewed it as an attempt to shore up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It would not be an indication that the Bank (of Canada) is trying to tighten policy," said Paul Ferley, assistant chief economist at the Bank of Montreal. \par \par \uc2  However, other economists argued that such a short-term view may lead to further weakness if speculators smell blood and savage the Canadian dollar again in the next few days. \par \par \uc2  "That will probably leave them exposed to further downward pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as speculators perhaps get a sense that the Bank wants to mount a defence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Citibank Canada economist Andrew Spence. \par \par \uc2  The fall in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  prompted a 12-basis-point rise in the Bank of Canada's key lending rate, the rate that guides banks' commercial and retail rates, to 7.24 percent. \par \par \uc2  Royal Bank's Wright said the rate move could be the Bank of Canada's attempt to pre-empt a widely expected rise in U.S. rates by the Federal Reserve at the end of January. \par \par \uc2  "Today (the Bank of Canada) moved ahead of the Fed," Royal Bank of Canada economist Craig Wright said. "Last time around they waited and waited and moved after the Fed." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1995 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011103dr1b08aoz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
