\par\par \b \uc2  CDN Afternoon: CDN Dlr Weakens On Fiscal Concerns \b0\par\par\uc2 526 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 1995\par 22:39\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1995, Dow Jones & Co., Inc.) \par \par \uc2  TORONTO (AP-DJ)--The Canadian dollar weakened in North American trading Monday and is sharply below levels sen late Friday after Canadian fiscal concerns were brought to the surface by Swedish and Mexican woes. The drop reportedly brought the Bank of Canada into the market selling U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar was trading at 1.4083 CDN dlrs around 2115 GMT Monday, up from 1.4028 CDN dlrs at 1300 GMT, when North American trading began, and up from 1.3995 CDN dlrs late Friday. \par \par \uc2  'Political and deficit concerns in other high debt countries such as Mexico, Italy and Sweden have heightened market sensitivities to the Canadian fiscal situation,' said Martin McCambridge, an economist for Royal Bank of Canada. McCambridge said in a report Monday, 'international investors appear to be sorting countries into two groups, those without political and deficit difficulties, and those with uncertainties. Unfortunately, Canada falls in the wrong group.' \par \par \uc2  There was selling of Canadian dollars out of Europe early Monday on news of a poll by COMPAS Inc. that shows 47% of Quebecers support sovereignty, up from 41% in a previous polls. \par \par \uc2  Traders on Chicago's IMM and New York-based investment banks began to sell Canadian dollar late in the session on bearish sentiment towards North America. The U.S. dollar also dropped Monday against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Mexican peso weakened Monday and the New York Federal Reserve Bank announced it was buying pesos against the U.S. dollar at the request of Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Banco de Mexico. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Canada\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't reported to be involved in this \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Concerns with Sweden's ability to hit defict targets in Tuesday's budget were in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market late Monday and led to negative market sentiment on Canada; Sweden and Canada face similar problems. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar broke through a resistance level at 1.4065 CDN dlrs Monday and traders said this triggered stop-loss selling that led to the test of 1.4080 CDN dlrs. The U.S. dollar is expected to trade in a range with support at 1.4040 CDN dlrs and resistance at 1.4090 CDN dlrs heading into Tuesday's session. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, the U.S. producer price index is expected early Tuesday, and strong inflationary signals from the u.S. will raise concerns about higher U.S. rates and undermine the Canadian dollar. Market expectations are for a 0.3% rise in the PPI in December, and a 0.2% climb in core PPI, which excludes volatile food and energy prices. \par \par \uc2  In Canadian economic news, hoousing starts for December will be released, and a survey by North American Economist is calling for a 1.4% decline from November. There is no major Canadian economic news on Monday. \par \par \uc2  These are \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in Toronto at 2115 GMT Monday compared with rates at 1300 GMT and late Friday. U.S./CDN               1.4083      1.4028     1.3995 CDN/Mark               1.0916      1.1108     1.1163 CDN/Yen                 70.90       71.89      72.37 \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 09-01-95 \par \par \uc2  2139GMT \par \par \uc2  1158 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011025dr19002bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
